# Everything in this repo is text and must stay byte-identical across clones.
#
# `overrides/*.patch` are unified diffs with three lines of context. Git on
# Windows defaults to converting text to CRLF on checkout; a diff converted that
# way no longer applies to the LF tree it was generated from, and `git apply`
# fails with "patch does not apply". That failure only shows up in a fresh
# clone — exactly the situation a sync happens in.
#
# A per-extension allowlist was wrong twice. First it missed `.patch`, then it
# missed `.sh`, `.lock` and `.tsv`, and each miss silently made a stranger's sync
# produce a different tree from the one this repo committed. So the default is now
# `text eol=lf` for every path, and only genuinely binary content opts out.
#
# `sync-skills.py` renormalises to LF as a second guard. Both are cheap and they
# fail differently, which is the point.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Built output is compared byte-for-byte by the seam test. `-text` stops git from
# touching it at all, which is the only safe setting for a compiled bundle.
lib/** -text

# Image and archive content: never normalised.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
